    "created_at": "2020-03-30T16:23:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-03-30T16:24:32Z",
    "body": "> Do we want to standardize further on clicking some non-interactive but still labelable descendant of label elements?\r\n\r\nI agree the answer is yes.\r\n\r\n> Which behavior do we want to standardize? Chrome/WebKit's or Firefox's?\r\n\r\nI agree the Chrome/WebKit behavior makes more sense. /cc @smaug---- in case he has a dissenting opinion.\r\n\r\n> In jsdom/jsdom#2921, @domenic mentioned some other possibilities, like making the activation behavior fully specified through an algorithm, with a possibly optional step of actually forwarding the click. This probably requires additional research.\r\n\r\nThanks for doing a bit of additional research. Given that, I agree that fully specifying is probably not the way to go. However we could probably go halfway. Instead of extending the spec's current form, which is\r\n\r\n> The label element's exact default presentation and behavior, in particular what its activation behavior might be, if anything, should match the platform's label behavior. The activation behavior of a label element for events targeted at interactive content descendants of a label element, and any descendants of those interactive content descendants, must be to do nothing.\r\n\r\nwe could do something like:\r\n\r\n> The activation behavior of a label element _label_, given event _event_, is given by the following steps:\r\n>\r\n> 1. If the _event_'s target is an interactive content shadow-including descendant of _label_, then return.\r\n> 1. If the _event_'s target is a shadow-including descendant of an interactive content shadow-including descendant of _label_, then return.\r\n> 1. If the labeled control is a shadow-including inclusive ancestor of _event_'s target, then return.\r\n> 1. Perform any platform-specific behavior for activating the label.\r\n\r\n(BTW, whether these conditions should be shadow-including or not is not obvious and probably needs web platform tests...)",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
